<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Kreuznach</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kreuznach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Kreuznach is a spa town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. It was the centre of the Front County of Sponheim and was later shared between the Electoral Palatinate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. Its brine springs made it a spa from the 19th century, and in 1917–1918 it was the seat of the German army's supreme headquarters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-kreuznach.jpg" alt="Bad Kreuznach" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alte_Nahebr%C3%BCcke_in_Bad_Kreuznach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bad Kreuznach lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> and the North Palatine Uplands, at the mouth of the Ellerbach where it empties into the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a>, about 14 kilometres south-southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingen-am-rhein">Bingen am Rhein</a>. It stands in the Nahe wine region, known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riesling">Riesling</a>, Silvaner and Müller-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a> wines. The town is the seat of its district and acts as the administrative, cultural and economic centre of a region with more than 150,000 inhabitants. Its best-known landmark is the Alte Nahebrücke, a medieval bridge from around 1300 that carries houses built between 1582 and 1612.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is evidence of a Celtic settlement here as early as the 5th century BCE. About 58 BCE the area became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and a Roman vicus arose on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, named according to legend after a Celt called Cruciniac. About 250 CE a very large luxurious villa-style palace was built, and in 370 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> had an auxiliary castrum built to help defend the frontier against Alemannic raids. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s fall, Kreuznach became a royal estate in the Frankish realm around the year 500, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> stayed at its royal palace in 819 and 839.</p><p>In the 11th century the settlement passed to the High Foundation of Speyer and shortly after 1105 to the Counts of Sponheim. They built the castle Kauzenburg between 1206 and 1230, and a New Town grew up on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a>'s north bank. Kreuznach received town rights, market rights and tolling rights in 1235 and 1270, and withstood a siege by Archbishop-Elector Baldwin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> in 1320. In 1375 the townsfolk rose against the town council, and Count Walram I of Sponheim had four leaders beheaded. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was introduced in 1557. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> married Jenny von Westphalen in the Pauluskirche on 19 June 1843.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bad Kreuznach is a spa town, advertised as the world's oldest radon-brine spa, and a centre for the care of rheumatic illness. Its baths contain the noble gas radon, and graduation towers stand in the Salinental. Winegrowing is also important: its 777 hectares of vineyard make it the largest winegrowing centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> region. Its economy rests mainly on small and medium businesses, with chemical, optical and machine-building industries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bad Kreuznach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 13th century the Rhineland county was divided into the Front County, centred on <b>Kreuznach</b>, and the Rear County, centred on Trarbach.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a></li></ul></section>
